 "verdicts": {
  "AIOS centralizes all request queues in the scheduler module rather than distributing them across processing modules, so each module focuses only on execution.": 1,
  "AIOS encapsulates LLMs as 'cores', akin to CPU cores, through a unified interface that integrates diverse LLM endpoints.": 1,
  "AIOS enforces isolation by preventing agents from directly manipulating resources, which also relieves agents of resource management.": 1,
  "AIOS has a memory manager for runtime agent data, a storage manager for persistent storage, a tool manager that loads tools and resolves call conflicts, and an access manager that implements access control and user intervention protocols.": 1,
  "AIOS implements two classic scheduling algorithms: First-In-First-Out (FIFO), which processes calls in arrival order but may increase waiting times for later requests, and Round Robin (RR), which cycles through calls in time slices.": 1,
  "AIOS reports up to 2.1x faster execution when serving agents built by various agent frameworks.": 1,
  "AIOS schedulers derive from a BaseScheduler class, so FIFO, Round Robin and priority-based schedulers can be added without modifying existing implementations.": 1,
  "AIOS splits the system into an application layer and a kernel layer, with the AIOS kernel isolating resources and LLM-specific services from agent applications.": 1,
  "AIOS's Round Robin scheduling depends on a context interrupt mechanism for LLM inference.": 1,
  "In an AIOS ablation (ReAct agents, HumanEval, Llama-3.1-8b), FIFO gave the shortest overall execution time, and RR ranked second in overall execution and average waiting time because context switching adds overhead.": 1,
  "In the AIOS kernel, agent queries are decomposed into sub execution units called AIOS syscalls to facilitate parallelism.": 1,
  "In the same ablation, Round Robin performed better than FIFO on p90 agent waiting time because its fairer scheduling reduces the chance that later tasks wait longer.": 1,
  "The AIOS SimpleContextManager enables preemptive multitasking by preserving LLM states across generation phases, with time-aware interruption.": 1,
  "The AIOS context manager has two approaches: text-based (for closed-source LLMs without logits access, saving decoded outputs) and logits-based (preserving the intermediate search tree structure for finer-grained state restoration).": 1,
  "The AIOS context manager supports task interruption and resumption through snapshot and restoration, to stop long-running LLM inference syscalls from monopolizing resources.": 1,
  "The AIOS kernel provides scheduling, context management, memory management, storage management, and access control services for runtime agents.": 1
 }
